1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,590
Can a tankless water heater keep up with your house?

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:03,765
Here's a quick test.

3
00:00:03,885 --> 00:00:07,220
A tankless heater warms water only as fast as it flows.

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,594
ENERGY STAR lists typical flow rates.

5
00:00:09,714 --> 00:00:12,844
A shower uses about two point five gallons per minute.

6
00:00:12,964 --> 00:00:14,626
A kitchen sink, two point two.

7
00:00:14,746 --> 00:00:16,717
A clothes washer, three point three.

8
00:00:16,837 --> 00:00:19,124
Run a shower and a sink at the same time,

9
00:00:19,244 --> 00:00:22,052
and you're asking for four point seven gallons per minute.

10
00:00:22,172 --> 00:00:28,005
An ENERGY STAR gas tankless heater only has to deliver at least two point eight gallons per minute,

11
00:00:28,125 --> 00:00:30,491
at a sixty-seven degree temperature rise.

12
00:00:30,611 --> 00:00:32,586
So at the minimum, it may not keep up.

13
00:00:32,706 --> 00:00:36,672
If that's how your house uses hot water, you'd need a larger unit.

14
00:00:36,792 --> 00:00:40,326
Before you buy, add up everything that runs at the same time.

15
00:00:40,446 --> 00:00:43,868
Then ask: can a tankless water heater keep up with your house?
